# 3. Surviving Question and Claim Ceiling

**Proposed single-sentence Gate 1 question (the required reframe):**  
On a narrow, reused or minimally extended epistemic-game family that already possesses (or can be given) explicit inspectable semantics and a separately auditable judge, and after confirming English renderings faithfully denote the formal instances, does a trigger-matched strategy pack improve verified accuracy on valid-move (S1) instances relative to a strong length-and-content-matched generic-method control, and does the inclusion of explicit obligations reduce misapplication errors on invalid-move (S2) instances relative to the identical pack without obligations?

**Narrow claim ceiling:**  
A positive result supports only that this specific external pack format (trigger + obligations) adds value over the chosen controls on the pre-specified family under the registered verification regime. It does **not** establish general retrieval mechanisms, recursive ToM competence, theory-of-mind as such, superiority of any particular semantics, or transfer beyond the frozen family. Strategy-pack value and obligation efficacy are separate claims and require separate primary contrasts. Accuracy does not imply the model executed the card’s reasoning steps.

**Q-A disposition:** Reclassify as a Gate-1/Gate-2 precondition (“select and audit one reusable family + independent judge + text-fidelity sample”) whose deliverable unlocks the empirical Q-B. Do not treat “what minimal specification” as the research question.

